<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/13th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Toluid Civil War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1260">1260</a>–<a href="/years/1264">1264</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Toluid Civil War was a succession war fought from 1260 to 1264 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a> and his younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a>, both grandsons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> through Tolui. Kublai's victory secured his position as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> treat the conflict as part of the dynasty's foundation, while it also deepened the fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> into separate khanates.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The family of Tolui had placed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a> on the throne at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultais</a> of 1250 and 1251, and after a failed plot by the Ögedeid candidate Shiremun and his cousin Nakhu, Möngke purged members of the Chagatai and Ögedei lines. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> then took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-baghdad-1258">sack of Baghdad</a> in 1258 angered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a>, its khan and a convert to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Möngke died in 1259 without naming a successor; he probably favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a>, whom he had made commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> in 1258.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Kublai was campaigning against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Southern Song</a> in 1260 when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> challenged him. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Kaiping</a> elected Kublai, the first to proclaim a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-khan">Great Khan</a> outside the Mongol homeland or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, and a month later a kurultai at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> proclaimed Ariq Böke. Most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>'s family and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a> backed Ariq Böke; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hulagu-khan">Hulagu</a> alone backed Kublai, but he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berke">Berke</a> were soon fighting each other. Kublai, drawing on the supplies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, cut taxes, adopted Chinese institutions and the era name Zhongtong, moderate rule, while his allies Kadan and Lian Xixian closed Ariq Böke's supply lines.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> lost the battle of Shimultai in 1261 and fought a stalemate near the Khingan Mountains ten days later. His ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alghu">Alghu</a>, whom he had made khan of Chagatai, refused him tax revenues and executed his envoys, and Ariq Böke turned on him, taking Almalikh. His supporters deserted, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongke">Möngke</a>'s son Ürüng Tash gave his father's tamga seal to Kublai. Ariq Böke travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shangdu">Shangdu</a> alone and surrendered in 1264.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Kublai imprisoned his brother and purged his sympathisers, executing the official Bolghai and others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ariq-boke">Ariq Böke</a> died mysteriously in prison in 1266, and some suspected Kublai of poisoning him. None of the other three khans attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> that confirmed Kublai, and from 1269 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaidu">Kaidu</a> of the Ögedei line fought him for decades. The four khanates became functionally independent; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ilkhanate">Ilkhanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> kept close ties, but the khanates never again fought together.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Toluid Civil War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The tension fed the <b>Toluid Civil War</b> with his younger brother Ariq Böke (1260–1264) and the long conflict with his kinsman Kaidu in Central Asia, which helped divide the Mongol world into rival khanates.</blockquote><a href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…left the Empire with two absentee kings during what historians call the Great Interregnum; and after the death of the Great Khan Möngke in 1259 rival kurultais elected his brothers Kublai Khan and Ariq Böke, whose war, the <b>Toluid Civil War</b>, hastened the division of the Mongol Empire.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Weaknesses</a></li><li><blockquote>His brothers Kublai and Ariq Böke were each proclaimed Great Khan by rival assemblies in 1260, and the <b>war between them</b> lasted until 1264.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-collapse-problem">The Collapse Problem</a></li></ul></section>
